                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Map the Room: who enters, and how deep`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `The Board has ordered a 2026–2031 strategy in 90 days, and a volunteer has already leaked the memo. Inclusion is the <strong>first dial</strong> of open strategy — and it has a cost curve. Assign each of the nine stakeholder groups a level: <strong>co-create</strong> (deep, ~£120K & 4 wks each), <strong>consult</strong> (~£45K & 1.5 wks), <strong>inform</strong> (~£8K), or <strong>exclude</strong> (free, but risky). Your cap is <strong>£600K and 10 weeks</strong>. Co-create everyone and you design a £1.9M / 22-week process you cannot deliver by 15 June. But exclude the wrong group — core editors, or the 7.1M small donors who give 87% of revenue — and you trigger the revolt. <em>Be deep where salience is highest; be light, not silent, everywhere else.</em>`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Set the Glass: transparency by phase`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Transparency is the <strong>second dial</strong>, independent of inclusion. Set openness for each phase — problem framing, option generation, financial modelling, recommendation. Full transparency maximises legitimacy but exposes half-formed options; the 2021 closed plan was rejected 71% and remembered as betrayal. The trap is the <strong>£20M AI-licensing figure</strong>: publish it raw in week 2 and it becomes the only story, three blogs cry “selling out,” and 400 editors organise. <em>Open the generation, protect the synthesis</em> — publish the figure only once conditions are attached, and pick channels that gather broadly but synthesise in structure.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Reconcile the Noise: choose, don’t average`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `The consultation returns 740 proposals clustering into four irreconcilable camps: <strong>purist</strong> (refuse all AI and commercial revenue), <strong>license</strong> (£20M/yr to AI firms), <strong>premium tier</strong>, and <strong>status-quo</strong>. You cannot adopt all four — a plan that says yes to everyone has no strategy in it, and the Board’s first question is “what are you NOT doing?” Build one spine of <strong>at most three priorities</strong>, settle the AI-licensing question, and tell the rejected camps they were <em>heard but not adopted</em> — reconciliation requires rejection, done legitimately.`,
