                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_overreach:     `<strong>You invited too many groups too deep.</strong> Your participation budget was £600K and 10 weeks — this configuration needs far more, and you will either blow the 15 June date or run a co-creation you cannot actually deliver. Which groups can be ‘consult’ or ‘inform’ without losing legitimacy? Inclusion has a cost curve; openness must be rationed.`,
                    coach_r1_thin:          `<strong>This reads as a closed process with a participation fig-leaf.</strong> Almost no one is co-creating and you’re barely spending the budget you were given. The community will see a token consultation, and that is how the 2021 plan started before it was rejected 71%.`,
                    coach_r1_excludecore:   `<strong>You excluded the core editors.</strong> 1,200 of them produce 41% of all content, and 34% would reduce or stop contributing if shut out of a major decision. A six-month editor slowdown cut donations £5.5M last time. This is the one exclusion most likely to trigger a revolt — reverse it.`,
                    coach_r1_excludesmall:  `<strong>You excluded the 7.1M small donors because they don’t edit.</strong> They also provide 87% of your £92M revenue. They may not want to co-create, but they will absolutely react to “Atlas sells your data to AI firms” in the press. Inform them — or read about your own strategy in the Guardian first.`,
                    coach_r1_targeted:      `<strong>That is a costed, salience-weighted room.</strong> You went deep where power and legitimacy are highest, kept the Board in the loop on its own deadline, informed the donors who fund you, and stayed within budget and the 10 weeks. Now you can actually deliver the process you designed.`,
                    coach_r2_premature:     `<strong>You published the £20M figure before any conditions were attached.</strong> It is now the only number anyone is discussing, three blogs have called it ‘selling out,’ and 400 editors are organising. Transparency on a half-formed option created the very crisis you were trying to avoid. Figures become public at recommendation, not at framing.`,
                    coach_r2_timing:        `<strong>Right timing on the figure.</strong> Holding the £20M until conditions, ring-fencing and a community veto are attached means it lands as a designed option, not a raw provocation. That is phase-dependent transparency — open the framing, protect the half-formed numbers.`,
                    coach_r2_mixed:         `<strong>That channel mix respects the wisdom-of-crowds boundary.</strong> Crowds generate superior idea variety but poor idea selection — so you opened generation through the forum and surveys, and you’ll synthesise through structured panels. Broad in, focused out.`,
                    coach_r2_forum:         `<strong>An open forum alone gives you noise, not a plan.</strong> 600–900 proposals with no structured synthesis is how an open process drowns. Open generation is good; you still need a structured channel to close the synthesis.`,
                    coach_r2_panels:        `<strong>Invited panels only reads as a closed inner circle.</strong> The quality may be high, but a process that only hears its own hand-picked voices is exactly the 2021 pattern the community remembers — and it will cost you legitimacy.`,
                    coach_r2_closedrec:     `<strong>A closed final recommendation is the 2021 betrayal in miniature.</strong> You can protect the half-formed financials, but if the community never sees the recommendation before the Board does, the process reads as theatre and legitimacy collapses.`,
                    coach_r2_never:         `<strong>Never publishing the financials protects coherence but reads as hiding the ball.</strong> The community knows the AI offer exists — the leaked memo named it. Total opacity on the number is its own admission; the question is when, not whether.`,
                    coach_r3_all:           `<strong>Your plan keeps the project pure, licenses to AI, launches a premium tier and cuts costs — simultaneously.</strong> The Board’s first question is “what are you NOT doing?” and you have no answer. A plan that says yes to everyone has no strategy in it. Which two camps did you actually choose?`,
                    coach_r3_toomany:       `<strong>Six priorities is not a strategy, it’s a wish list.</strong> The Board’s focus test asks what you are deliberately not doing. Cut the spine to two or three priorities you can defend, sequence the rest, and reject the others openly.`,
                    coach_r3_statusquo:     `<strong>“Change nothing, just trim” fails the brief.</strong> Reader growth is flat for the first time and AI assistants are eating the traffic that funds you. Cost-cutting buys time, not a future — the Board ordered a five-year strategy, not a holding pattern.`,
                    coach_r3_conditional:   `<strong>A conditional yes on the £20M is the integrative move.</strong> Ring-fencing the revenue for editor tools with a community veto on terms captures the money without reading as ‘selling out’ — it holds the plan and the relationship instead of trading one for the other.`,
                    coach_r3_accept:        `<strong>Accepting the £20M with no conditions hands the purists their exit.</strong> The revenue is real, but unconditioned it confirms every ‘Atlas sells out’ fear — and the editors who produce 41% of content are exactly the ones who leave. Attach conditions or expect the slowdown.`,
                    coach_r3_refuse:        `<strong>Refusing the £20M protects legitimacy but leaves a hole.</strong> You’ve honoured the purists, but reader growth is flat and AI is eroding the donation base — where does the new revenue line the Board demanded come from now? Refusal is defensible; just don’t pretend the revenue problem went away.`,
                    coach_r3_heard:         `<strong>The “you were heard” log is what makes rejection legitimate.</strong> Naming each rejected camp and showing where its idea went — or why it was dropped — closes the dialogic loop. They lost the argument, but not their standing in the process, and that is what protects their continued contribution.`,
                    coach_r3_silence:       `<strong>Rejecting camps in silence is how you turn losers into enemies.</strong> Dialogic strategy confers legitimacy through the conversation, not just the outcome. Tell the camps you dropped that they were heard, or they will conclude the consultation was theatre.`,
                    coach_r4_both:          `<strong>Pitching the focus AND the open process is how you bring the community trustees with you.</strong> The three elected trustees are watching whether you treat the vote as content alone — show them the plan is sharp and the room was open, and you can win approval without losing them.`,
                    coach_r4_content:       `<strong>You won the Board 4–3 with a brilliant plan — but the three community-elected trustees voted against.</strong> Within the hour the forum reads “imposed from above.” You have approval and a legitimacy crisis. In an open organisation, winning the vote against the community is a loss, not a win.`,
                    coach_r4_legitonly:     `<strong>All process and no substance fails the Board’s focus test.</strong> Legitimacy without a coherent plan is a well-run conversation that produced nothing the Board can approve — and missing 15 June means the external consultancy writes it instead.`,
                    coach_r4_capitulate:    `<strong>You dropped the AI clause the moment 600 editors threatened a freeze.</strong> That is 0.25% of your editors setting five-year strategy by ultimatum. What signal does instant capitulation send for the next contested decision? Was there a conditional version — revenue ring-fenced for editor tools, a community veto on terms — that held the plan AND the relationship?`,
                    coach_r4_ignore:        `<strong>Ignoring the freeze threat outright treats a real constituency as noise.</strong> 600 editors may be a fraction, but a public freeze plus the donation lag is a £5.5M risk. Holding firm without a conversation is how a loud faction becomes a movement — engage the threat, don’t dismiss it.`,
                    coach_r4_held:          `<strong>That holds the plan and the relationship at once.</strong> Ring-fencing the revenue for editor tools and granting a community veto on terms answers the freeze without surrendering the clause — you reconciled the conflict instead of capitulating to it. This is dialogic conflict resolved, not avoided.`,
                    coach_r4_nogov:         `<strong>A plan with no governance mechanism won’t survive to 2031.</strong> Approval today is not implementation through five years — without a standing community-and-board role, the next contested decision reopens the whole fight. Commit the mechanism that carries it.`,
                    coach_r4_spin:          `<strong>Claiming a “fully co-created plan” you didn’t deliver is the fastest way to lose the legitimacy you built.</strong> The community knows exactly how open the process was — overclaiming reads as spin and erases the trust an honest account would have kept.`,
                    coach_r4_topdown:       `<strong>“The Board has approved the strategy,” full stop, reads as imposition.</strong> After an open process, a top-down announcement tells the community their voice ended at the vote. Show them what they shaped and what you chose — the statement is part of the legitimacy, not an afterthought.`,
                    coach_r4_landed:        `<strong>That is open strategy landed.</strong> A pitch that won the split Board on focus and process, a conditional answer that held the freeze, a standing governance council, and an honest closing statement — you secured formal approval and informal legitimacy together. Neither alone would have held.`,

